 |year=1989
 |publisher= Parliamentary Research Services
 |location=Chichester
 |isbn= 0-900178-27-2
 |page=215
}}</ref>

==Campaign==
The campaign was "short but exceptionally keen".<ref name="times38564">{{cite news |title=Election Intelligence; Worcester |work=The Times |issue=38564 |date=8 February 1908 |location=London |page=10}}</ref> Goulding campaigned for [[Tariff Reform League|tariff reform]] and had the support of most of [[Worcester City Council]].<ref name="times38564"/> The 1906 election court case had to be paid for by the [[county borough]], which duly increased the [[rates (tax)|rates]], causing resentment against the Liberals as the party whose previous candidate had lodged the petition.<ref name="times38564"/> There was also an [[anti-incumbency]] sentiment against the [[Liberal government, 1905–1915|Liberal government]], which was blamed for food price inflation.<ref name="times38564"/>

